<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#well">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Well</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as wells</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A well is a shaft dug into the ground to reach water, essential for life and travel in arid regions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>. Control of wells determined which routes caravans and armies could use. The construction of wells along routes enhanced the operational effectiveness of its armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/well.jpg" alt="Well" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Konouyawo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puiser_de_l%27eau_dans_un_puit_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
